WEBVTT

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:05.400
There’s English bread, German bread, and French bread,

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.280
but Japan’s bread, <i>Japan</i>, does not exist.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.620
In that case, there’s no choice but to create it.

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:12.900
This story is a serious, biographical ballad of a boy who possesses Solar Hands,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:18.170
Azuma Kazuma, who will create a Japanese bread made by and for the Japanese people,

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:21.840
which can be presented to the world proudly.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:39.060
Somewhere far away, there are people listening carefully.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:47.030
In every place, there are people looking up at the sky.

00:00:48.150 --> 00:00:54.910
Under the night sky, we, who are whistling...

00:00:55.950 --> 00:01:02.620
without words, are just tracing constellations with our fingers.

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:10.090
The dreams of the cold-sensitive... Your cold hands...

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:19.470
The warming magic is to believe in one path.

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:28.230
The single star found on the other side of the comet cloud...

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:32.490
whether it be a shining or a faint star...

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.450
is the light of only you.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:39.230
On the other side of the cloud in your heart,

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:44.040
is a sign post that remains unseen.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.760
Now, opening these hands, what will you believe in today?

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.510
Azuma Kazuma and Kawachi Kyousuke have started working at the southern Tokyo branch

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:01.930
of Pantasia, Japan’s number one bakery chain...

00:02:01.930 --> 00:02:04.220
However, they had been ordered
by the manager, Matsushiro Ken,

00:02:04.220 --> 00:02:08.070
to make bread that a horse would find delicious.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:11.440
Have a taste of delicious bread, made Kawachi-style.

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:14.900
Oats, huh?

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.700
Delicious!

00:02:19.150 --> 00:02:21.660
On the other hand,
the bread Kazuma made...

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:27.740
It’s made with butter from Hanako, a goat’s milk.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.660
Goat butter!

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.170
It’s <i>Japan</i> #24’s variant,

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:34.460
France-<i>Japan</i>!

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:40.470
Kansai-ben.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:42.050
Give me his bread.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.810
De-licious!

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.530
This is...

00:02:52.190 --> 00:02:55.730
de-licious!

00:02:56.400 --> 00:02:58.400
However, the horse wouldn’t eat it.

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.110
I was particular about the “delicious bread,”

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:04.490
but you made a bread that exceeded my expectations.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.910
That is... de-licious bread!

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:10.420
An interesting pair has arrived this year...

00:03:10.420 --> 00:03:11.300
And...

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.540
to think that one possesses Solar Hands...

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:18.260
My store will be interesting from now on.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.060
It’s the Main Store!!

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:22.860
Dancing Meister!

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:32.770
This is a relay from the main store of Pantasia,

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:34.480
the leading bakery chain in the country of Japan.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.160
Once you eat bread from Pantasia,
you’ll feel like eating some more.

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.830
This time, they are announcing a new type of bread,

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:41.740
Welcome.
This time, they are announcing a new type of bread,

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.370
and there are long lines of people
wanting to try it right away.

00:03:45.370 --> 00:03:49.170
Pantasia’s new creation, in terms of wine,
is equivalent to Beaujolais Nouveau.

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:51.750
Ahhh, I want to try it soon, too!

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:55.460
Man, what’s with all this excitement
over the main store?

00:03:55.460 --> 00:03:57.090
Compared to that...

00:03:57.090 --> 00:04:02.220
Why is this southern Tokyo branch so quiet?
Is this really a branch of the same store?

00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.350
We only get around 4 or 5 customers a day...

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.140
Don’t you agree, Azuma?

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:07.600
Wha–?

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.900
What are you doing?
What’s that mountain of bread?

00:04:12.900 --> 00:04:15.070
I’m going to use it for <i>Japan</i> #57.

00:04:16.030 --> 00:04:17.070
R-Rock solid!

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:19.410
What do you mean by #57?

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.830
As if there’s any use for this hard leftover bread.

00:04:22.830 --> 00:04:24.990
This hardness is what’s good.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:27.620
Dumbass! You should throw out
these leftovers.

00:04:27.620 --> 00:04:28.830
Why?

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.040
As it is,

00:04:30.040 --> 00:04:33.330
this lodging room on the store’s second floor
is packed enough with just the two of us.

00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:37.090
Hey Azuma, think about it...

00:04:37.090 --> 00:04:40.590
The amount of these left-overs...
Do you understand what it means?

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.140
It’s showing us how much the store is in danger!

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:46.090
We’re finished if we don’t get any
customers, even with your <i>Japan</i>!

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:48.390
It’ll all get thrown away!

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.310
T-That’s terrible!

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:52.560
What should we do, Kawachi?

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:58.190
In any case, we need to ask the manager and Tsukino about this tomorrow...

00:04:58.190 --> 00:05:00.360
The management policy of this store, as well as

00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:02.490
our guaranteed wage, and everything else.

00:05:02.490 --> 00:05:03.620
You’re right.

00:05:07.760 --> 00:05:09.460
I-I’m sorry for asking such an impertinent question!

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:12.170
It’s just that Azuma
really wanted me to ask!

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:12.910
Eh?!

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:15.170
Ouch.

00:05:15.170 --> 00:05:18.840
You really want to know why our store isn’t prospering?

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:20.720
No, it’s okay, I’m sorry...

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.890
Please have a look in the
2 o’clock direction.

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:27.060
To our store, this is the root of all evil...

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:31.890
This is Pantasia’s biggest rival, the St. Pierre
bakery chain’s main Tokyo store!

00:05:32.810 --> 00:05:35.650
Something like that is looming over
there, right in front of Pantasia.

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:36.940
It’s natural for us to
not get any customers.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.990
W-Why didn’t I notice this before?!

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.570
Such a huge store is diagonally opposite from ours...

00:05:47.910 --> 00:05:50.290
Well well, if it isn’t the people of Pantasia,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:52.080
out and about in the morning.

00:05:52.710 --> 00:05:54.120
Mokoyama...

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:57.340
Perhaps you’re practicing for a play?

00:05:57.340 --> 00:05:58.670
Yeah, something like that.

00:05:58.670 --> 00:05:59.330
No, we’re not.

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:02.550
Manager, who’s that?

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.920
The manager of St. Pierre’s main Tokyo store...

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:07.630
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi.

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:08.310
I see.

00:06:08.310 --> 00:06:09.970
That manager over there...

00:06:09.970 --> 00:06:12.890
Yet another scary guy has appeared,
ready to cause a commotion.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.520
Good morning, manager over there!

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:18.190
Oh, what a cute young boy.
Are you new?

00:06:18.190 --> 00:06:19.150
Good morning.

00:06:19.630 --> 00:06:20.110
Huh?

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:22.740
Is that bread you’re holding?

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:26.000
It looks horrible, what exactly is it?

00:06:26.870 --> 00:06:29.660
It’s the beginning of my new bread creation, <i>Japan</i> #57.

00:06:29.660 --> 00:06:31.200
New bread creation?

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:32.550
<i>Japan</i>?

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:35.120
Is that a pun?

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.340
Oh no, that’s terrible!

00:06:37.340 --> 00:06:39.420
I dislike puns!

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:42.090
Moreover... that naming is lame.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.010
A sense of style is important in bread-making.

00:06:44.010 --> 00:06:46.670
If you’re like this at a young age,
your future prospects are bleak.

00:06:47.930 --> 00:06:51.310
Azuma, your <i>Japan</i> is really being made fun of.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.390
Oh? I thought so, too.

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:55.690
Hey! Don’t make fun of <i>Japan</i>!

00:06:55.690 --> 00:07:00.100
<i>Japan</i> was the name it was given by a bakery
oji-san who I met back when I was 6!

00:07:00.100 --> 00:07:02.190
Ohhh, you poor thing!

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:05.570
You caught your lameness
from that oji-san?

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:11.120
It would’ve been better had you met
a fine adult when you were young.

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:15.210
For instance, a bread artisan with
fabulous sense of style, like me...

00:07:17.250 --> 00:07:18.380
You bastard...

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.500
Don’t make a fool of Oji-san, or <i>Japan</i>...!

00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:21.510
Calm down now.
Don’t make a fool of Oji-san, or <i>Japan</i>...!

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:23.820
There’s no sense in getting worked up

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:25.960
over cheap taunts from an artisan of only Mokoyama’s level.

00:07:26.470 --> 00:07:28.390
Of only my level, you say?

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.010
What is that supposed to mean, Ken-chan?

00:07:30.010 --> 00:07:31.470
Don’t call me Ken-chan.

00:07:31.890 --> 00:07:34.650
Well, I’ll put it simply...

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:38.560
This kid has only just started with us, but, Mokoyama...

00:07:38.560 --> 00:07:42.520
with the kind of bread you make,
you cannot beat this boy’s <i>Japan</i>!

00:07:42.520 --> 00:07:43.990
What did you say?!

00:07:43.990 --> 00:07:45.520
Try saying that again!

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.940
I’ll say it as many times as you want.

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:52.740
An artisan of your level is no match for this boy!

00:07:54.500 --> 00:07:58.580
Even if it’s Ken-chan, I cannot forgive
any more rude remarks!

00:07:58.580 --> 00:07:59.510
Stop calling me Ken-chan...
Even if it’s Ken-chan, I cannot forgive
any more rude remarks!

00:07:59.510 --> 00:08:00.000
Stop calling me Ken-chan...

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:03.380
But what’s with that confidence?

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:05.470
This is beyond irritating!

00:08:06.010 --> 00:08:08.550
He’s saying that that kid
is superior to me,

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:11.420
the manager of St. Pierre’s
main Tokyo store?

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:12.930
It’s a bluff.

00:08:12.930 --> 00:08:14.680
If has to be a bluff!

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:15.680
That’s it!

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:22.610
Actually, we are about to have a presentation
of our new brand of bread.

00:08:22.610 --> 00:08:26.070
Reporters from a TV station will be covering
the event, as a special program.

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:28.570
Well? Are you just boasting?

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.700
Well, there’s a bit of that, but...

00:08:30.700 --> 00:08:35.790
If you wish, I will allow you to present
your <i>Japan</i> on that program as well.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:40.040
I shall humiliate you, in front of the whole country!

00:08:40.040 --> 00:08:41.790
Are you stupid?

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:45.800
As if I’d accept such a boring invitation.

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:47.750
If we’re going to do this...

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.250
it would have to be a new bread competition,
or else there’s no excitement.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.600
Is Pantasia’s new bread, <i>Japan</i>, superior?

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:56.640
Or is St. Pierre’s new bread superior?

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.010
Let’s settle it... on TV.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:00.510
TV?!

00:09:01.150 --> 00:09:03.560
What a bold, fearless man...

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:05.440
Beautiful!

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:07.940
I accept the challenge!

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:10.520
This is why you’re my Ken-chan!

00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:12.530
A man among men!

00:09:12.990 --> 00:09:15.200
He said “my...”

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:17.080
What kind of relationship do you have?

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:19.530
Like I’d know.
It’s best not to think about it.

00:09:19.960 --> 00:09:21.580
The presentation will be the day after tomorrow.

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:25.340
In the meantime, try to think of a
bread that won’t embarrass you.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:33.590
So that’s the story. Keep it a secret from the main store, Tsukino.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:34.640
Umm...

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.380
Somehow, I feel we can’t do that...

00:09:37.890 --> 00:09:41.400
I do have some faith in <i>Japan</i>, but...

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:45.940
Still, <i>Japan</i> itself is not one of
Pantasia’s brands of bread.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:50.610
And yet, we are betting Pantasia’s
reputation on this competition...

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:52.190
This is quite a problem.

00:09:52.530 --> 00:09:54.400
All we have to do is win.

00:09:54.780 --> 00:09:58.740
It’s easy to say that, but our opponent
is the manager, isn’t he?

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.290
He’s a skilled artisan
in his own way, isn’t he?

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.660
Do you really expect that we’ll win?

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:05.160
Nope!

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.140
After all,

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.170
Mokoyama is a first-class artisan who’s even known as the “God of sweetened buns.”

00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:13.880
His personality is, well, the way
it is... but his skill is genuine.

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:16.630
G-God?

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.640
The new bread would most likely be
a sweetened bun, his specialty.

00:10:21.230 --> 00:10:23.190
G-God, no way...

00:10:23.190 --> 00:10:27.140
Let’s suppose Azuma’s <i>Japan</i> were
to lose, what would happen?

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:28.820
Well...

00:10:29.690 --> 00:10:32.730
This store would be ruined...
because of <i>Japan</i>.

00:10:34.180 --> 00:10:35.100
Ah, yes...

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:37.900
By the way, that <i>Japan</i> #57...

00:10:38.790 --> 00:10:39.860
Is it tasty?

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:42.210
I can’t take any more!

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.840
We might be ruined!

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:48.790
Why is this?!

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.000
W-What is it?

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.220
They’ve got to be kidding me.

00:10:55.220 --> 00:11:00.320
For tomorrow’s special program, I wanted
to appear in the costume I designed...

00:11:01.690 --> 00:11:03.560
But the response from the organizers...

00:11:03.560 --> 00:11:05.190
was a negative!

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:07.020
This is just terrible.

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:12.240
Well, never mind.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.400
What’s happening with you lot?

00:11:14.400 --> 00:11:15.520
What about tonight’s beauty treatment?

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:17.520
Yes. You are booked in.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:19.280
And the nail salon after that?

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.780
Booking for a first-rate job, confirmed.

00:11:21.780 --> 00:11:22.990
My onion perfume?

00:11:22.990 --> 00:11:24.200
We have it stocked.

00:11:24.200 --> 00:11:25.240
Hairstyling?

00:11:25.240 --> 00:11:27.450
Of course, with a “charisma hairdresser.”

00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.790
A charming-man, I hope?

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:30.790
That I would really not know...

00:11:32.580 --> 00:11:34.500
I-I shall confirm it right away!

00:11:36.500 --> 00:11:39.930
Now I can comfortably face tomorrow’s battle.

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.260
And the most crucial part...?

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.390
Yes! Here it is.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.850
This very high-grade sugar...

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:49.720
What exactly do you have in mind?

00:11:49.720 --> 00:11:51.270
This is what I’ll do.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:01.740
Such a large quantity...

00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:04.700
Are you out of your mind, Manager?

00:12:04.990 --> 00:12:08.740
If you put it in like that, the excess
sugar will cause it to burn...

00:12:11.500 --> 00:12:14.670
You! You’re too noisy for a mere worker!

00:12:14.670 --> 00:12:15.420
How is it?

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:17.330
The taste of my new bread...

00:12:17.330 --> 00:12:19.210
Although it’s only a trial...

00:12:23.050 --> 00:12:25.310
So sweet!

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.720
What an excellent reaction.

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:33.930
With this, my victory is certain.

00:12:33.930 --> 00:12:37.560
Just you wait, Ken-chan. Just you wait, Pantasia.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:41.740
I’ll make you regret having ever challenged
Mokoyama Tsuyoshi, the genius.

00:12:53.870 --> 00:12:55.040
Why?

00:12:56.710 --> 00:12:58.040
Why what?

00:12:58.630 --> 00:13:02.260
Nothing, I just couldn’t settle down
without asking a sharp question.

00:13:02.260 --> 00:13:03.920
I mean, come on!

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.300
The new bread showdown is tomorrow, you know!

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:10.140
On top of that, if we lose, this store could be ruined!

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.600
Well, just eat this and relax.

00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:15.070
Ah yes, I haven’t eaten anything
all morning.

00:13:20.520 --> 00:13:22.860
This is the hard leftover bread
from before!

00:13:22.860 --> 00:13:25.430
Quit collecting these things and throw them out!

00:13:25.430 --> 00:13:27.530
I can’t let any food go to waste, can I?

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:30.790
I know!

00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:33.150
As a variation of the croissant you made before,

00:13:33.150 --> 00:13:36.290
make a 648 layer Danish, or something like that.

00:13:36.780 --> 00:13:40.450
We might stand a chance with that.
They’ll all bend over backwards!

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:42.450
What’s a Danish?

00:13:42.450 --> 00:13:44.130
You don’t know again?

00:13:44.130 --> 00:13:46.390
Well, don’t worry, Kawachi.

00:13:46.390 --> 00:13:48.100
It’s okay, it’ll be delicious...

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:50.270
<i>Japan</i> #57.

00:13:50.270 --> 00:13:50.810
Probably.

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:53.560
“Probably” isn’t good enough!

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:56.860
But, this is the first time
I’m making #57...

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:58.570
It’s a new type of <i>Japan</i>.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:13:59.560
Never mind.

00:14:00.110 --> 00:14:02.950
We’ve got the manager,
then dumbass Azuma,

00:14:02.950 --> 00:14:04.660
and an unreliable senpai...

00:14:04.660 --> 00:14:06.070
H-How rude!

00:14:06.070 --> 00:14:08.210
What’s happening with this store?

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:11.370
And then there’s Tsukino.

00:14:11.370 --> 00:14:14.510
On weekdays, she’s only here in the
early morning and in the evening.

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:16.460
What’s up with that?

00:14:16.460 --> 00:14:18.920
Is she still the manager’s proxy? Geez...

00:14:18.920 --> 00:14:20.550
Oh? You don’t know...

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:21.170
Don’t know?

00:14:21.170 --> 00:14:23.420
Everyone! This is terrible!

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:26.340
Outside...

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.920
Outside, it’s terrible...

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:32.430
Who is it?!

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:33.430
What’s that outfit?

00:14:34.600 --> 00:14:36.440
You’re a student?

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:38.190
Never mind that, outside...!

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:41.810
St. Pierre

00:14:48.570 --> 00:14:49.010
Note: The posters say “St. Pierre vs. Pantasia New Bread Showdown.”

00:14:49.010 --> 00:14:50.580
W-What the hell is this?

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:52.780
They’ve even put posters on our store...

00:14:53.370 --> 00:14:56.080
He likes to put on a show,
that Mokoyama...

00:14:56.080 --> 00:14:56.790
Manager!

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:02.050
Looks like there’s no backing out now.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:08.510
Oh dear.

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:12.640
Tsukino, you... told on us, didn’t you?

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:15.010
No. I’m sorry to say that I haven’t yet...

00:15:17.030 --> 00:15:18.020
It’s the main store!

00:15:23.530 --> 00:15:25.360
Hey, it’s Kuroyanagi from the main store.

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:36.250
Matsushiro-san.

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:39.670
You have... committed yet another selfish act.

00:15:39.670 --> 00:15:40.330
I have?

00:15:41.380 --> 00:15:43.130
It’s no use playing the fool.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:47.590
Great Tokyo TV and St. Pierre’s P.R.
department have both contacted us.

00:15:48.720 --> 00:15:51.090
Everyone likes to gossip, eh?

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:55.350
In any case, regarding this matter, the
main store will be taking charge.

00:15:55.350 --> 00:15:58.020
And as for your punishment, expect to hear from us.

00:15:58.440 --> 00:16:01.560
It seems you’re annoying as always, boy.

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:03.730
If I’m gonna argue... I’ll do it with him.

00:16:04.070 --> 00:16:06.310
He’s here... isn’t he?!

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:11.360
What?

00:16:24.550 --> 00:16:26.080
Meister Kirisaki.

00:16:28.670 --> 00:16:30.550
W-Who’s this?

00:16:30.550 --> 00:16:33.760
A man of even higher standing in the
Pantasia Group than my ojii-sama,

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.470
the only person who’s been given the title of Meister...

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:39.810
General Manager of the main store, Meister Kirisaki.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:46.360
I’m not sure why, but I hear he seriously
dislikes making public appearances.

00:16:46.900 --> 00:16:49.360
This is the first time I’ve seen him as well.

00:16:59.370 --> 00:17:02.290
I wish to meet Azuma-kun...

00:17:02.290 --> 00:17:03.540
Meister Kirisaki!

00:17:03.540 --> 00:17:05.000
Allow me to...

00:17:17.680 --> 00:17:19.690
This young boy is...

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.030
Azuma Kazuma-kun.

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:23.690
Meister! Please wait!

00:17:23.690 --> 00:17:25.610
Hey! You stay out of this!

00:17:25.610 --> 00:17:26.940
What did you say?

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.190
Oh?

00:17:28.190 --> 00:17:33.150
He does not notice the noise outside,
or even my presence.

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:37.410
Delicious!

00:17:37.410 --> 00:17:38.910
Looks good.

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:41.590
Old bread...

00:17:42.620 --> 00:17:43.620
What exactly...?

00:17:47.630 --> 00:17:49.600
What is that inside the pot?

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:51.630
You surprised me!

00:17:51.630 --> 00:17:53.630
W-What are you? A bird?

00:17:54.550 --> 00:17:56.180
Oh, you’re a person...

00:17:56.180 --> 00:17:57.350
What is in the pot?

00:17:57.350 --> 00:18:00.350
Ah, this? This is, you know...

00:18:01.020 --> 00:18:03.100
Sauce for my new type of <i>Japan</i>.

00:18:03.600 --> 00:18:04.820
Sauce?

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:07.100
Is that so?

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:10.070
By the way,
what is the price of that bread?

00:18:10.650 --> 00:18:11.570
Price?

00:18:11.570 --> 00:18:13.950
I haven’t really thought about it.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:17.200
In that case, please give it some thought.

00:18:17.200 --> 00:18:18.380
O-Okay.

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:21.250
Meister!

00:18:21.250 --> 00:18:22.330
Meister Kirisaki!

00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:25.080
Matsushiro-san...

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.830
I had heard the rumors, but it seems you have
really obtained an interesting new employee.

00:18:28.830 --> 00:18:30.420
I suppose so.

00:18:30.420 --> 00:18:32.090
But I won’t hand him over to you.

00:18:33.880 --> 00:18:35.090
Matsushiro-san.

00:18:35.090 --> 00:18:38.050
What price do you have in mind
for that bread?

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:41.980
To put a price on that bread...

00:18:41.980 --> 00:18:45.060
I don’t think... I have the courage.

00:18:45.480 --> 00:18:48.320
I can sympathize with that...

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:52.060
Though while I say it,
I am equally guilty.

00:18:52.820 --> 00:18:54.280
We are leaving now, Kuroyanagi.

00:18:54.280 --> 00:18:57.070
But... Meister Kirisaki...

00:19:20.970 --> 00:19:22.390
So late!

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:27.100
Why does he always cut things so close?!

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.310
Doesn’t he know that today’s
the day of the settlement?

00:19:30.770 --> 00:19:32.570
Now now, Kawachi-san.

00:19:32.570 --> 00:19:35.650
I’m sure Azuma-san is hurrying.

00:19:35.650 --> 00:19:36.820
Are you sure?

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:39.780
Argh! Look at the time already.

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:41.910
It’d be a disaster if we didn’t make it

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:45.000
to the presentation because <i>Japan</i> wasn’t completed.

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:47.890
But yes, if it so happens that we cannot make it to the presentation,

00:19:47.890 --> 00:19:49.080
I wonder what would happen...

00:19:50.290 --> 00:19:52.590
If we don’t make it to the presentation...

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:53.930
We run for our lives...

00:19:54.760 --> 00:19:56.800
Major bashing...

00:19:56.800 --> 00:19:58.840
Farewell to the southern Tokyo store...

00:19:58.840 --> 00:20:00.420
Suddenly unemployed...!

00:20:00.420 --> 00:20:02.310
The changing times!

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:03.300
Ahh, this is no good.

00:20:03.720 --> 00:20:06.810
Right! Now that it has come to this,
I’ll go and drag him out.

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:08.150
Just wait a minute.

00:20:08.150 --> 00:20:08.900
Eh?

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:10.610
Eat this, and calm down.

00:20:11.770 --> 00:20:13.520
That’s right, I haven’t had breakfast yet.

00:20:15.520 --> 00:20:16.650
What, this again?

00:20:20.070 --> 00:20:21.240
Preparation complete!

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:23.290
What’ve you been doing?

00:20:23.290 --> 00:20:25.040
Don’t get so mad.

00:20:25.040 --> 00:20:27.280
I was making <i>Japan</i> #57.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:29.210
I know that much.

00:20:29.210 --> 00:20:30.920
If you know, then don’t ask.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:31.710
What did you say?

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:33.000
Umm...

00:20:33.500 --> 00:20:35.840
It would be best if we left now...

00:20:35.840 --> 00:20:36.760
Oh crap!

00:20:36.760 --> 00:20:38.630
Looks like we’ve got to run, Azuma!

00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:40.510
Ah, please wait!

00:20:41.600 --> 00:20:43.510
Relax, Kawachi...

00:20:44.180 --> 00:20:46.770
Normally... one would go by car.

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.240
Am I forgotten?

00:20:55.820 --> 00:20:58.820
TV Grand Prix! I’m your hostess, Ooe Mariko.

00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:03.330
Today we had planned to broadcast St. Pierre’s
presentation of their latest bread series,

00:21:03.330 --> 00:21:04.990
but the plans have been changed.

00:21:04.990 --> 00:21:08.160
Instead, the two giants that reign
at the top of the bread industry,

00:21:08.160 --> 00:21:14.000
St. Pierre and Pantasia, are set for a showdown of their latest bread creations.

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:16.000
Young boy...

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:20.720
It’s unfortunate, but defeating
my masterpiece is impossible.

00:21:20.720 --> 00:21:24.260
I’d like to give you this handkerchief, for when you lose.

00:21:24.260 --> 00:21:25.890
<i>Japan</i> will win.

00:21:25.890 --> 00:21:28.590
And that handkerchief smells kind of strange.

00:21:28.590 --> 00:21:32.060
Oh! You don’t understand the
greatness of onion perfume?!

00:21:32.060 --> 00:21:33.520
Well never mind, just watch...

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:35.730
I’ll pay no attention when you cry later.

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:40.620
First, representing St. Pierre, we
present Mokoyama Tsuyoshi.

00:21:42.780 --> 00:21:44.740
They say that two giants cannot stand together.

00:21:44.740 --> 00:21:48.240
Here, in this place,
I shall bring Pantasia to its end.

00:21:51.080 --> 00:21:56.500
Now, representing Pantasia,
Azuma Kazuma!

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:57.500
H-Hello.

00:22:01.050 --> 00:22:03.340
I-It’s finally started...

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:06.810
It seems the important guy from the main
store approved of Azuma’s bread...

00:22:06.810 --> 00:22:10.520
<i>Japan</i> #57... It just might work out, right?

00:22:11.810 --> 00:22:14.020
No. It may not work.

00:22:16.230 --> 00:22:22.400
To tell the truth... Azuma’s bread,
<i>Japan</i> #57, has a fatal flaw.

00:22:22.400 --> 00:22:24.830
F-Fatal flaw?

00:22:39.540 --> 00:22:47.680
If I think of you, and even utter the clichéd phrase “I love you,”

00:22:48.250 --> 00:22:56.690
it makes such a nice sound, and you continue talking.

00:23:00.260 --> 00:23:07.700
The hopeless tears your bold self sometimes shows me form flag of truce.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:12.870
If nothing is said, then all the more,

00:23:13.070 --> 00:23:19.010
I just can’t look away. Things like sadness and pain...

00:23:19.010 --> 00:23:21.580
Well, that happens a lot in life, after all.

00:23:21.810 --> 00:23:25.720
When you casually say, “Do your best,” it gives me strength.

00:23:26.150 --> 00:23:30.490
Whenever your small back trembles, I will embrace you right away.

00:23:31.520 --> 00:23:34.330
Even if the scenery outside my window changes...

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:39.030
Stay with me as you are, bad sleeping habits and all.

00:23:39.660 --> 00:23:44.200
If you sulk, and utter

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:53.010
Even the clichéd “I hate you,” it comes across as the opposite.

00:23:53.410 --> 00:24:02.370
I will love you, no matter what. It’s just another Sunday to me.

00:24:06.980 --> 00:24:09.620
Oh my, Hattori-sensei of the judging panel...

00:24:09.620 --> 00:24:11.360
I shall be indebted to you.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:13.590
They brought the real Hattori-sensei?!

00:24:13.590 --> 00:24:15.490
Next episode, Yakitate!! Japan.

00:24:15.490 --> 00:24:17.080
Hattori Surprise!!

00:24:17.080 --> 00:24:19.000
Transformation With the Secret Sauce!

00:24:19.000 --> 00:24:20.420
Be sure to see it!

00:24:21.370 --> 00:24:23.760
Yakitate!! Seed of knowledge.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:27.380
High-grade sugar, shirozaratou, has
large grains and is hard to use.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:29.550
However, the refreshingly refined sweetness

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:32.740
brings out the natural taste of the bread ingredients!

00:24:33.140 --> 00:24:34.520
Wow!
